    "docs/DECISION_LOG.md": {
     "66": "## 10. HMPE creep at Rev A sustained load — named hard part [Likely — creep guidance is secondary-sourced (MP-001), spot-check pending]",
     "68": "**Date 2026-09-02 · Status OPEN · Sources: `docs/research/mp001_market_pricing.md` §3c; `docs/research/lr001_splice_literature.md` §D.**",
     "70": "At SF 2.5 the HMPE bulk sections carry a steady centrifugal load of 28–40% of rated break strength (40% raw; 28% if the 30% strength derate is applied in sizing). Published HMPE guidance is to keep sustained load at or below ~20% of break for multi-year life (SK78 ≈ 8 years at 20% load, 20 °C). The centrifugal load is genuinely sustained in the rotating frame, so this is the unfavourable regime.",
     "72": "**Consequence:** Reaching 20% sustained load would require SF ≈ 5, which cuts characteristic velocity by √2 and drives the 2.5 km/s reference mass ratio from ~41× to order ~800× under the Moravec form. That is a refutation of the design, not a variant of it. SF 2.5 therefore stands, and the creep exposure is carried as a named hard part rather than designed away.",
     "74": "**Admissible outcomes (not yet decided):** (a) creep-governed tether replacement cadence, costed into ops; (b) hybrid material boundary shifted toward PBO for the high-stress fraction; (c) HMPE grade change — DM20-class is reported to creep >50× slower than SK78 (LR-001 §D), so grade may matter as much as geometry; (d) some mix of the above.",
     "76": "**Evidence that would close or change this entry:** ST-002 coupon creep-rate ratio at ~20% vs ~40% of break (4–8 week hold); the TP-001 creep test series; a rope-maker application-engineering dataset on DM20 vs SK78 sustained-load life; a primary-source creep curve replacing the secondary guidance above."
    },
    "docs/project_instructions.md": {
     "10": "A skyhook, for the purposes of this project, is a **rotating momentum-exchange tether**: a very long, very strong cable in orbit around Earth, spinning end-over-end around its own center of mass while that center of mass orbits the planet. The rotation is timed so that at the bottom of each swing, the lower tip of the tether dips deep into the upper atmosphere (or close to it) while moving, relative to the ground, far slower than orbital velocity. A vehicle launched from Earth only needs to reach that slow-moving tip, grab on, and then be flung up and forward as the tether continues to rotate — gaining altitude and velocity it never had to pay for with rocket propellant. Half an orbit and half a rotation later, the payload is released at the top of the swing with a velocity *above* orbital, free to coast to higher orbits, the Moon, or interplanetary trajectories.",
     "67": "| Dyneema SK99 (UHMWPE) | ~3.6 GPa | 970 kg/m³ | ~3.7 MJ/kg | ~2.7 km/s |",
     "108": "- **Architecture:** Hoytether-style redundant multi-line ribbon or tubular truss of primary load lines with diagonal cross-links. **Construction baseline (adopted from HASTOL Phase I, verified in VM-001):** Spectra/Dyneema-class UHMWPE for the bulk of each arm, with the outer ~20 km of the lower arm in heat-resistant Zylon for the aerothermal regime. Minimum redundancy: survive complete severance of any 25% of primary lines at any cross-section while carrying full design load at remaining safety factor ≥ 1.5.",
     "112": "- **Environmental protection:** Zylon degrades badly under UV and atomic oxygen. Every load-bearing fiber gets a protective jacket (metallized coating and/or aluminized polymer over-braid). Requirement: ≤ 10% strength loss over 10 years at operational altitude, validated by accelerated AO/UV/thermal-cycling chamber testing and by exposure coupons flown on early missions."
    },
    "docs/research/mp001_market_pricing.md": {
     "22": "| HMPE (SK78/SK99) specs: 0.97 g/cm³, SK99 ≈ +15–20% strength vs SK78, melt 145 °C | — | — | — | — | secondary spec sheets |",
     "23": "| HMPE creep guidance: sustained load ≤ ~20% of break for multi-year life; SK78 ≈ 8 yr at 20% load, 20 °C; creep 3–5× aramid | — | — | — | — | secondary engineering guidance |",
     "46": "**3c · Creep vs SF 2.5 — a newly quantified named problem [Likely].** At SF 2.5 the HMPE bulk sections carry a **steady centrifugal load of 28–40% of rated break strength** (40% raw; 28% if the 30% strength derate is applied in sizing). Public guidance: keep sustained HMPE load **≤ ~20%** for multi-year life (SK78 ≈ 8 yr at 20% and 20 °C). The centrifugal load is genuinely sustained in the rotating frame — this is exactly the bad regime. Reaching 20% load needs SF ≈ 5, which cuts Vc by √2 and blows the reference mass ratio from **~41× to order ~800×** (Moravec form) — not a design, a refutation. So the honest outcomes are: (a) HMPE sections have a **creep-governed replacement cadence** costed into ops; (b) the **hybrid boundary shifts toward PBO** (aramid-class creep, 3–5× lower) for the high-stress fraction; or (c) some mix. This goes on the risk register with numbers, feeds the TP-001 creep series, and motivates **ST-002** (below). Per Rule 6, this joins the named hard parts."
    },
    "docs/research/lr001_splice_literature.md": {
     "45": "**D · Creep couples straight into MP-001 §3c.** Published HMPE creep concern rises above ~15–20% of break load; **DM20 grade creeps >50× slower than SK78**; offshore uses creep safety factors ~5–8. Our Rev A HMPE sections sit at 28–40% steady load — squarely in the bad regime — so **material grade (DM20-class) may matter as much as splice geometry**, and ST-002's coupons test exactly this. Feeds TP-001 and the risk register."
    },
    "physics/tether_dynamics.py": {
     "17": "Mass and stiffness discretization [Certain]:",
     "18": "  The continuum uniform-working-stress (taper-optimized) arm profile is",
     "19": "    A(s) = A_L * exp[x^2 * (1 - s^2/L^2)],   A_L = m_p * omega^2 * L / sigma_w",
     "20": "  with sigma_w = sigma*(1-derate)/SF, x = (v_tip/V_c)*sqrt(SF/(1-derate)),",
     "21": "  V_c = sqrt(2*sigma/rho) (EQ-4). This is the same profile whose integrated",
     "22": "  mass gives EQ-6 (Moravec/Forward): m_arm = m_p*sqrt(pi)*x*exp(x^2)*erf(x).",
     "23": "  Node lump masses are EXACT integrals of rho*A(s) over each half-segment",
     "24": "  window (via erf), so total fiber mass matches EQ-6 to machine precision.",
     "25": "  Analytic tension for verification: T(s) = sigma_w * A(s).",
     "81": "    payload_both_tips: bool = False   # attach design payload at both tips (V3)",
     "120": "        if cfg.payload_both_tips:",
     "353": "        measured at the catch instant. Gravity-gradient modulation (~4% at this",
     "357": "        self.m[0] += payload_kg"
    }
